WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.658
This lesson becomes much easier when components are treated as a new description of the same vector rather than as extra forces added to the scene.

2
00:00:06.658 --> 00:00:13.316
A diagonal push can be rewritten as horizontal and vertical parts on chosen axes.

3
00:00:13.316 --> 00:00:19.973
Once that is done, each axis can be handled cleanly with signs, and only after that should the final resultant be rebuilt.

4
00:00:19.973 --> 00:00:26.631
This is the core discipline of vector components.

5
00:00:26.631 --> 00:00:33.289
Keep this takeaway in view: Components are a cleaner description of one vector, not extra forces added on top.

6
00:00:33.289 --> 00:00:39.947
Original arrow means one force.

7
00:00:39.947 --> 00:00:46.604
The diagonal vector is the real starting force story.

8
00:00:46.604 --> 00:00:53.262
Arrow Split means component description.

9
00:00:53.262 --> 00:00:59.920
Horizontal and vertical parts describe that same vector on chosen axes.

10
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:06.284
The arrow-split diagram shows the central move of the lesson: rewrite the diagonal force as perpendicular horizontal and vertical components.

11
00:01:06.284 --> 00:01:12.649
The original vector has not changed.

12
00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:19.013
Only its description has changed.

13
00:01:19.013 --> 00:01:25.377
This matters because axis bookkeeping is much easier with straight horizontal and vertical pieces than with diagonal arrows that must be compared all at once.

14
00:01:25.377 --> 00:01:31.741
Keep this takeaway in view: Resolve first so the axis bookkeeping becomes simple and trustworthy.

15
00:01:31.741 --> 00:01:38.106
Same vector: The diagonal force and its components describe the same underlying push.

16
00:01:38.106 --> 00:01:44.470
Chosen axes: Components depend on the axes chosen for the problem.

17
00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:51.799
Once the net horizontal and net vertical components are known, the final resultant is rebuilt as a diagonal from those perpendicular totals.

18
00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:59.127
This is why plain same-line algebraic addition is not enough here.

19
00:01:59.127 --> 00:02:06.456
Perpendicular components need vector addition, which leads to a diagonal resultant.

20
00:02:06.456 --> 00:02:13.784
The geometry of that diagonal is what brings in Pythagoras and the direction angle.

21
00:02:13.784 --> 00:02:21.113
Keep this takeaway in view: Perpendicular component totals lead to a diagonal resultant, not a plain arithmetic sum.

22
00:02:21.113 --> 00:02:28.441
Horizontal total: Combine left-right components with signs on the x-axis first.

23
00:02:28.441 --> 00:02:35.770
Vertical total: Combine up-down components with signs on the y-axis first.

24
00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:40.854
A strong analogy is a city-grid route.

25
00:02:40.854 --> 00:02:45.937
A diagonal trip can be described as a certain number of east steps and a certain number of north steps.

26
00:02:45.937 --> 00:02:51.021
The destination is the same either way.

27
00:02:51.021 --> 00:02:56.105
Only the bookkeeping changed.

28
00:02:56.105 --> 00:03:01.188
That is exactly what components do for a diagonal force.

29
00:03:01.188 --> 00:03:06.272
The vector stays the same, but the axis description becomes cleaner and easier to combine with other axis-aligned quantities.

30
00:03:06.272 --> 00:03:11.355
Keep this takeaway in view: Arrow Split is a cleaner axis description of one original vector.

31
00:03:11.355 --> 00:03:16.439
Diagonal trip means original vector.

32
00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:21.523
This is the full move before it is split.

33
00:03:21.523 --> 00:03:26.606
East and north steps means components.

34
00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:31.690
These are the axis descriptions of the same move.

35
00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:39.088
If the net horizontal and net vertical components are perpendicular, the resultant magnitude comes from Pythagoras.

36
00:03:39.088 --> 00:03:46.486
The direction is then measured counterclockwise from the plus x-axis in this lesson.

37
00:03:46.486 --> 00:03:53.884
In first-quadrant cases, the angle size can be found with tan inverse of y over x.

38
00:03:53.884 --> 00:04:01.282
But the signs of the net components still matter, because they tell you which quadrant the resultant actually lies in.

39
00:04:01.282 --> 00:04:08.680
Keep this takeaway in view: First combine components with signs, then use geometry to rebuild the final vector.

40
00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:16.078
Magnitude: Use Pythagoras only after the net axis components are known.

41
00:04:16.078 --> 00:04:23.476
Direction: Measure counterclockwise from the positive x-axis in this lesson.

42
00:04:23.476 --> 00:04:30.874
resultant magnitude = sqrt(x^2 + y^2).

43
00:04:30.874 --> 00:04:38.272
theta = tan^-1(y / x) in first-quadrant cases.

44
00:04:38.272 --> 00:04:45.670
Use the net x and net y totals after signed component combination, then measure direction from +x.

45
00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:52.660
A common trap is to add eight and six and say the resultant must be fourteen.

46
00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:59.650
That would only work for same-line arrows.

47
00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:06.640
Another trap is to forget the sign of one component and lose the correct quadrant.

48
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:13.630
A third trap is to measure the direction from the y-axis even though the lesson has defined the angle from the positive x-axis.

49
00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:20.620
These are all bookkeeping errors, and the cure is the same: resolve first, combine by axis, then rebuild carefully.

50
00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:27.610
Keep this takeaway in view: Component questions are won by disciplined bookkeeping, not by rushing the final arrow.

51
00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:34.600
Resolve the diagonal vector before combining it with other forces.

52
00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:41.590
Combine horizontal and vertical parts separately with signs.

53
00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.820
Check yourself.

54
00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:48.050
What should happen first with a diagonal vector?

55
00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:51.280
Resolve it into components on chosen axes.

56
00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.510
What do you combine next?

57
00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.740
Net horizontal and net vertical components separately with signs.

58
00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:00.970
What comes only after that?

59
00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:04.200
The final resultant magnitude and direction.

60
00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.430
Those three steps are the entire routine of the lesson.

61
00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.660
Keep this takeaway in view: Resolve, combine, rebuild: that is the full component routine.

62
00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.890
First means resolve.

63
00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:17.120
Rewrite the diagonal vector as component parts.

64
00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.350
Second means combine by axis.

65
00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:23.580
Keep x and y bookkeeping separate and signed.

66
00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:28.909
You are ready for the worked example now.

67
00:06:28.909 --> 00:06:34.239
First resolve the diagonal force into eight newtons east and six newtons north.

68
00:06:34.239 --> 00:06:39.568
Then combine one axis at a time.

69
00:06:39.568 --> 00:06:44.897
Horizontally, eight east and three west leave five newtons east.

70
00:06:44.897 --> 00:06:50.226
Vertically, the six newtons north stays unchanged.

71
00:06:50.226 --> 00:06:55.556
Now rebuild the final resultant from those perpendicular totals.

72
00:06:55.556 --> 00:07:00.885
Its magnitude is the square root of five squared plus six squared, which is the square root of sixty-one, about seven point eight newtons.

73
00:07:00.885 --> 00:07:06.214
Because both net components are positive, the resultant points northeast.

74
00:07:06.214 --> 00:07:11.544
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: resolve, combine, then rebuild the northeast resultant.

75
00:07:11.544 --> 00:07:16.873
Step 1: Resolve the diagonal force into horizontal and vertical components.

76
00:07:16.873 --> 00:07:22.202
Step 2: Combine net x and net y separately with signs.

77
00:07:22.202 --> 00:07:27.531
net x = 8 east - 3 west = 5 N east.

78
00:07:27.531 --> 00:07:32.861
net y = 6 N north.

79
00:07:32.861 --> 00:07:38.190
Combine by axis first, then rebuild the final vector from the perpendicular totals.
